\section{Lagrangian Formulation}

The dynamics of the inverted pendulum are derived using the
Euler--Lagrange formulation. The Lagrangian is defined as
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{L}=T-V,
    \label{eq:lagrangian_definition}
\end{equation}
where $T$ is the total kinetic energy and $V$ is the gravitational
potential energy.

For a system with generalized coordinates $q_i$, the
Euler--Lagrange equations are
\begin{equation}
    \frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t}
    \left(
        \frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}
        {\partial \dot{q}_i}
    \right)
    -
    \frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}
    {\partial q_i}
    =
    Q_i^{\mathrm{nc}},
    \label{eq:euler_lagrange}
\end{equation}
where $Q_i^{\mathrm{nc}}$ is the nonconservative generalized force
associated with the generalized coordinate $q_i$.

\subsection{Coordinate System and Definitions}

The system is described using an inertial Cartesian coordinate frame
$\mathcal{F}_I$. The $x$ axis is horizontal and positive to the right,
while the $z$ axis is vertical and positive upward.

The wheel axle is located at
\begin{equation}
    \bm{r}_{\mathrm{w}}
    =
    \begin{bmatrix}
        x\\
        z
    \end{bmatrix},
    \label{eq:wheel_position}
\end{equation}
where $z$ is treated as constant.

The center of mass of the pendulum body is located at
\begin{equation}
    \bm{r}_{\mathrm{b}}
    =
    \begin{bmatrix}
        x_{\mathrm b}\\
        z_{\mathrm b}
    \end{bmatrix}
    =
    \begin{bmatrix}
        x+\ell\sin\theta\\
        z+\ell\cos\theta
    \end{bmatrix}.
    \label{eq:body_position}
\end{equation}

The angle $\theta$ is measured clockwise from the positive $z$ axis.
Consequently, $\theta=0$ corresponds to the upright equilibrium
configuration of the inverted pendulum.

The generalized coordinates are selected as
\begin{equation}
    q_1=x,
    \qquad
    q_2=\theta,
    \qquad
    q_3=\phi_{\mathrm w},
    \label{eq:generalized_coordinates}
\end{equation}
where $\phi_{\mathrm w}$ is the angular position of the wheel. The
corresponding angular velocity is
\begin{equation}
    \omega_{\mathrm w}
    =
    \dot{\phi}_{\mathrm w}.
    \label{eq:wheel_angular_velocity}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Rolling Constraint}

Assuming pure rolling without slip, the translational and rotational
motions of the wheel are related by
\begin{equation}
    \dot{x}
    =
    r_{\mathrm w}\dot{\phi}_{\mathrm w},
    \label{eq:rolling_velocity_constraint}
\end{equation}
where $r_{\mathrm w}$ is the wheel radius.

Equivalently, the integrated constraint can be written as
\begin{equation}
    x-r_{\mathrm w}\phi_{\mathrm w}
    =
    C,
    \label{eq:rolling_position_constraint}
\end{equation}
where $C$ is a constant determined by the initial configuration.

The constraint is enforced using a Lagrange multiplier
$\lambda$. The corresponding constrained Euler--Lagrange equations
are
\begin{equation}
    \frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t}
    \left(
        \frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}
        {\partial\dot{q}_i}
    \right)
    -
    \frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}
    {\partial q_i}
    =
    Q_i^{\mathrm{nc}}
    +
    \lambda
    \frac{\partial f}{\partial q_i},
    \label{eq:constrained_euler_lagrange}
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
    f(q)
    =
    x-r_{\mathrm w}\phi_{\mathrm w}-C
    =
    0.
    \label{eq:constraint_function}
\end{equation}

With this convention, $\lambda$ corresponds to the horizontal
wheel--ground traction force.

\subsection{Kinetic Energy}

The total kinetic energy consists of the translational kinetic energy
of the wheel and axle assembly, the translational and rotational
kinetic energies of the pendulum body, and the rotational kinetic
energy of the wheel.

Differentiating \eqref{eq:body_position} gives
\begin{equation}
    \dot{x}_{\mathrm b}
    =
    \dot{x}
    +
    \ell\dot{\theta}\cos\theta,
    \qquad
    \dot{z}_{\mathrm b}
    =
    -
    \ell\dot{\theta}\sin\theta.
    \label{eq:body_velocity_components}
\end{equation}

Therefore, the squared speed of the pendulum-body center of mass is
\begin{align}
    v_{\mathrm b}^2
    &=
    \dot{x}_{\mathrm b}^{\,2}
    +
    \dot{z}_{\mathrm b}^{\,2}
    \nonumber\\
    &=
    \dot{x}^{\,2}
    +
    2\ell\dot{x}\dot{\theta}\cos\theta
    +
    \ell^2\dot{\theta}^{\,2}.
    \label{eq:body_speed}
\end{align}

The translational kinetic energy of the wheel and axle assembly is
\begin{equation}
    T_{\mathrm w,trans}
    =
    \frac{1}{2}m_{\mathrm w}\dot{x}^{\,2}.
    \label{eq:wheel_translational_ke}
\end{equation}

The translational kinetic energy of the pendulum body is
\begin{equation}
    T_{\mathrm b,trans}
    =
    \frac{1}{2}m_{\mathrm b}
    \left(
        \dot{x}^{\,2}
        +
        2\ell\dot{x}\dot{\theta}\cos\theta
        +
        \ell^2\dot{\theta}^{\,2}
    \right).
    \label{eq:body_translational_ke}
\end{equation}

The rotational kinetic energy of the pendulum body is
\begin{equation}
    T_{\mathrm b,rot}
    =
    \frac{1}{2}I_{\mathrm b}\dot{\theta}^{\,2},
    \label{eq:body_rotational_ke}
\end{equation}
and the rotational kinetic energy of the wheel is
\begin{equation}
    T_{\mathrm w,rot}
    =
    \frac{1}{2}I_{\mathrm w}\dot{\phi}_{\mathrm w}^{\,2}.
    \label{eq:wheel_rotational_ke}
\end{equation}

Therefore, the total kinetic energy is
\begin{align}
    T
    &=
    T_{\mathrm w,trans}
    +
    T_{\mathrm b,trans}
    +
    T_{\mathrm b,rot}
    +
    T_{\mathrm w,rot}
    \nonumber\\
    &=
    \frac{1}{2}
    (m_{\mathrm b}+m_{\mathrm w})
    \dot{x}^{\,2}
    +
    m_{\mathrm b}\ell
    \dot{x}\dot{\theta}\cos\theta
    \nonumber\\
    &\quad
    +
    \frac{1}{2}
    \left(
        I_{\mathrm b}
        +
        m_{\mathrm b}\ell^2
    \right)
    \dot{\theta}^{\,2}
    +
    \frac{1}{2}
    I_{\mathrm w}
    \dot{\phi}_{\mathrm w}^{\,2}.
    \label{eq:total_kinetic_energy}
\end{align}

\subsection{Potential Energy}

The gravitational potential energy of the pendulum body is
\begin{equation}
    V
    =
    m_{\mathrm b}g z_{\mathrm b}.
    \label{eq:gravitational_potential_general}
\end{equation}

Substituting \eqref{eq:body_position} gives
\begin{equation}
    V
    =
    m_{\mathrm b}g
    \left(
        z+\ell\cos\theta
    \right).
    \label{eq:gravitational_potential}
\end{equation}

Since $z$ is constant, the term $m_{\mathrm b}gz$ is a constant and
does not affect the equations of motion. Therefore, the potential
energy may be written as
\begin{equation}
    V
    =
    m_{\mathrm b}g\ell\cos\theta.
    \label{eq:potential_energy}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Lagrangian}

Combining the kinetic and potential energies gives
\begin{align}
    \mathcal{L}
    &=
    \frac{1}{2}
    (m_{\mathrm b}+m_{\mathrm w})
    \dot{x}^{\,2}
    +
    m_{\mathrm b}\ell
    \dot{x}\dot{\theta}\cos\theta
    \nonumber\\
    &\quad
    +
    \frac{1}{2}
    \left(
        I_{\mathrm b}
        +
        m_{\mathrm b}\ell^2
    \right)
    \dot{\theta}^{\,2}
    +
    \frac{1}{2}
    I_{\mathrm w}
    \dot{\phi}_{\mathrm w}^{\,2}
    -
    m_{\mathrm b}g\ell\cos\theta.
    \label{eq:lagrangian}
\end{align}

\subsection{Equations of Motion}

The motor applies equal and opposite torques to the wheel and
pendulum body. Thus, the nonconservative generalized forces associated
with the motor are
\begin{equation}
    Q_x^{\mathrm{nc}}=0,
    \qquad
    Q_\theta^{\mathrm{nc}}=-M_{\mathrm{mot}},
    \qquad
    Q_{\phi_{\mathrm w}}^{\mathrm{nc}}=M_{\mathrm{mot}},
    \label{eq:generalized_motor_forces}
\end{equation}
where $M_{\mathrm{mot}}$ is the motor torque.

\subsubsection{Axle Translation}

Applying the constrained Euler--Lagrange equation to $x$ gives
\begin{equation}
    (m_{\mathrm b}+m_{\mathrm w})\ddot{x}
    +
    m_{\mathrm b}\ell
    \left(
        \ddot{\theta}\cos\theta
        -
        \dot{\theta}^{\,2}\sin\theta
    \right)
    =
    \lambda.
    \label{eq:axle_equation}
\end{equation}

Because $\lambda$ represents the horizontal wheel--ground traction
force under the selected sign convention,
\begin{equation}
    F_{\mathrm f}=\lambda.
    \label{eq:traction_force}
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Pendulum-Body Rotation}

Applying the Euler--Lagrange equation to $\theta$ gives
\begin{equation}
    \left(
        I_{\mathrm b}
        +
        m_{\mathrm b}\ell^2
    \right)
    \ddot{\theta}
    +
    m_{\mathrm b}\ell
    \ddot{x}\cos\theta
    -
    m_{\mathrm b}g\ell\sin\theta
    =
    -M_{\mathrm{mot}}.
    \label{eq:body_angular_equation}
\end{equation}

The first term represents the angular inertia of the pendulum body,
the second represents translational--rotational coupling, and the
third represents the gravitational torque.

\subsubsection{Wheel Rotation}

Applying the constrained Euler--Lagrange equation to
$\phi_{\mathrm w}$ gives
\begin{equation}
    I_{\mathrm w}\ddot{\phi}_{\mathrm w}
    =
    M_{\mathrm{mot}}
    -
    r_{\mathrm w}\lambda.
    \label{eq:wheel_equation}
\end{equation}

Using the no-slip constraint,
\begin{equation}
    \ddot{\phi}_{\mathrm w}
    =
    \frac{\ddot{x}}{r_{\mathrm w}},
    \label{eq:wheel_acceleration_constraint}
\end{equation}
and therefore
\begin{equation}
    \lambda
    =
    \frac{M_{\mathrm{mot}}}{r_{\mathrm w}}
    -
    \frac{I_{\mathrm w}}{r_{\mathrm w}^{\,2}}\ddot{x}.
    \label{eq:traction_force_explicit}
\end{equation}

Thus, the wheel--ground traction force is not generally equal to
$M_{\mathrm{mot}}/r_{\mathrm w}$. The difference accounts for the
angular acceleration of the wheel.

\subsection{Reduced Equations of Motion}

Substituting \eqref{eq:traction_force_explicit} into
\eqref{eq:axle_equation} gives
\begin{align}
    \left(
        m_{\mathrm b}
        +
        m_{\mathrm w}
        +
        \frac{I_{\mathrm w}}{r_{\mathrm w}^{\,2}}
    \right)
    \ddot{x}
    +
    m_{\mathrm b}\ell\cos\theta
    \ddot{\theta}
    -
    m_{\mathrm b}\ell
    \sin\theta\,\dot{\theta}^{\,2}
    =
    \frac{M_{\mathrm{mot}}}{r_{\mathrm w}}.
    \label{eq:reduced_translation}
\end{align}

Together with the pendulum-body equation,
\begin{equation}
    \left(
        I_{\mathrm b}
        +
        m_{\mathrm b}\ell^2
    \right)
    \ddot{\theta}
    +
    m_{\mathrm b}\ell\cos\theta
    \ddot{x}
    -
    m_{\mathrm b}g\ell\sin\theta
    =
    -M_{\mathrm{mot}},
    \label{eq:reduced_rotation}
\end{equation}
the system dynamics are described by two coupled nonlinear
differential equations in $x$ and $\theta$.

These equations can be expressed in matrix form as
\begin{equation}
    \bm{M}(q)
    \begin{bmatrix}
        \ddot{x}\\
        \ddot{\theta}
    \end{bmatrix}
    +
    \bm{h}(q,\dot{q})
    +
    \bm{g}(q)
    =
    \bm{B}M_{\mathrm{mot}},
    \label{eq:matrix_dynamics}
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
    \bm{M}(q)
    =
    \begin{bmatrix}
        m_{\mathrm b}
        +m_{\mathrm w}
        +I_{\mathrm w}/r_{\mathrm w}^{\,2}
        &
        m_{\mathrm b}\ell\cos\theta
        \\[4pt]
        m_{\mathrm b}\ell\cos\theta
        &
        I_{\mathrm b}
        +m_{\mathrm b}\ell^2
    \end{bmatrix},
    \label{eq:mass_matrix}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
    \bm{h}(q,\dot{q})
    =
    \begin{bmatrix}
        -m_{\mathrm b}\ell
        \sin\theta\,\dot{\theta}^{\,2}\\
        0
    \end{bmatrix},
    \label{eq:centrifugal_vector}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
    \bm{g}(q)
    =
    \begin{bmatrix}
        0\\
        -m_{\mathrm b}g\ell\sin\theta
    \end{bmatrix},
    \label{eq:gravity_vector}
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
    \bm{B}
    =
    \begin{bmatrix}
        1/r_{\mathrm w}\\
        -1
    \end{bmatrix}.
    \label{eq:input_matrix}
\end{equation}

